      "reasoning": "Student is experiencing exam panic and needs calm de-escalation. Following the step_walkthrough strategy, I must calm them down, break the question down, and ask ONE diagnostic question without giving away the full answer.",
      "content": "Take a deep breath; you've got this, and we will take it one small step at a time. You don't need to know every single book in the novel, just the main themes of his library. \n\nLet's think about Arthur Jarvis as a character. He was an engineer who spent a lot of time reading deeply about the country he lived in. What major country or social issues do you remember him researching or reading about in his study?"
    },
    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Um... I remember he had books about South Africa, and maybe religion or social issues? Does that count?"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "reasoning": "Student correctly identified South Africa and religion/social issues. I will acknowledge their answer without unearned reflexive praise, provide the complete memo model answer, and include the mandatory structural sections.",
      "content": "Yes, South Africa and religion (or social issues/sociology) are correct. In *Cry, the Beloved Country*, Arthur Jarvis's library is a powerful symbol that reveals his deep intellectual and moral awakening regarding South Africa's socio-political realities.\n\n**Final Answer:** Any two topics, e.g., South Africa and religion (Acceptable alternatives: African history, race relations, sociology, crime and criminals, Abraham Lincoln, Afrikaans literature, poetry, or novels).\n\n---\n### 📖 Concept & Thought Process (How to Understand It):\n* **The Concept / Rule:** Arthur Jarvis's study represents his ideological shift and his desire to understand the systemic inequalities and struggles of South Africa, largely inspired by Abraham Lincoln and social justice movements.\n* **The Thought Process (How to Unpack It):** \n  1. Recall the significance of Arthur Jarvis's study after his death (James Jarvis enters and learns who his son truly was).\n  2. Identify the broad thematic categories of books present in his room (history, politics, social welfare, religion).\n  3. Select two clear, distinct topics that align directly with the novel's curriculum notes.\n* **How DBE Markers Score It:** 1 mark is awarded for each valid topic/subject correctly identified, up to a maximum of 2 marks. Answers must be specific to the novel's context.\n\n🎯 **Exam Tip:** When a question asks for \"TWO subjects/topics\", list them clearly as bullet points or separate sentences. Do not bundle them into a vague single sentence, so the marker can easily award 1 mark per distinct point."
    }
  ],
  "meta": {
    "strategy_used": "step_walkthrough",
    "final_answer": "Any two topics, e.g., South Africa and religion.",
    "verify_code": "",
    "techniques": [
      "context_clues",
      "mark_per_point"
    ]
  }
}

